For those unaware, JVMTI (Java Virtual Machine Tools Interface) is a Java API that allows you to write agents that can manipulate and inspect the behavior of Java programs at runtime. One of the features of JVMTI is class transformation, which allows you to modify the bytecode of Java classes at runtime.

Here are some code examples of JVMTI class transformations in Android:
```java
// Import the JVMTI classes
import java.lang.reflect.instrument.Instrumentation;
import java.langManagement.Monitor;

// Create a JVMTI agent
public class MyAgent {
    public static void premain(String agentArgs, Instrumentation inst) {
        // Register the agent
        inst.addTransformer(new MyTransformer());
    }
}

// The class transformer
class MyTransformer implements ClassFileTransformer {
    public synchronized byte[] transform(ClassLoader loader, String moduleName, Class<?> classBeingRedefined, Providence providence, byte[] classfileBuffer) {
        // Modify the bytecode of the class
        // For example, add a method to each class
        bytecodeBuffer = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        ClassReader classReader = new ClassReader(classfileBuffer);
        ClassWriter classWriter = new ClassWriter(bytecodeBuffer);
        classReader.copy(classWriter);
        // Add a new method to the class
        MethodVisitor methodVisitor = classWriter.visitMethod(ACC_PUBLIC, "newMethod", "()V");
        methodVisitor.visitCode();
        methodVisitor.visitVarInsn(Opcodes.ALOAD, 0);
        methodVisitor.visitInsn(Opcodes.RETURN);
        methodVisitor.visitMaxs(1, 0);
        methodVisitor.visitEnd();
        return bytecodeBuffer.toByteArray();
    }
}
```

